    "case_id": "479",
    "case_summary": "A 23-year-old woman presented with a 10-day history of vomiting, myalgia, and upper abdominal pain. Three days before presentation, she received clarithromycin from her general practitioner without improvement. Two days later, she developed jaundice and pruritus. Medical history includes ulcerative colitis with a pan-proctocolectomy and end ileostomy. She took no regular medication, rarely drank alcohol, and had no history of travel abroad or recent blood transfusions. On the third day of hospital admission, she developed bilateral parotitis and an itchy maculopapular morbilliform rash across her back and torso, which rapidly spread to the proximal aspects of all four limbs.\n\nPatient was jaundiced, with fever (38.0°C) and tachycardia (120 beats per minute). Other vital signs were normal. She had tenderness in the upper abdomen, but neither the liver nor spleen was palpable, and she had no signs of chronic liver disease. The stoma was pink and healthy. Oropharyngeal examination was normal, and she had no lymphadenopathy or organomegaly.\n\nLaboratory Tests:\n- ALT: 374 U/L (normal 10–36 U/L)\n- ALP: 326 U/L (normal 30–130 U/L)\n- Bilirubin: 108 μmol/L (normal <21 μmol/L)\n- C-reactive protein: 7.2 mg/L (normal <5 mg/L)\n- Haemoglobin: 151 g/L (normal 120–160 g/L)\n- White cell count: 18.1 × 10⁹ per L (normal 3.9–11.1 × 10⁹ per L)\n- Lymphocytes: 14.48 × 10⁹ per L (normal 1.0–3.2 × 10⁹ per L)\n\nImaging Studies:\n1. Transabdominal ultrasound:\n- Thick-walled gallbladder with multiple stones\n- No intrahepatic or proximal extrahepatic biliary duct dilatation\n- Enlarged spleen measuring >12 cm\n\n2. MRCP:\n- No evidence of choledocholithiasis\n- Confirmed splenomegaly\n\nSerological Tests:\n- Positive glandular fever screen\n- Negative EBV nuclear antigen IgG\n- Positive EBV viral capsid IgG antibody\n- Positive EBV viral capsid IgM antibody",
